    WFJ-15: compare the served bytes against the recorded digest
    
    The export-readable clause of WFJ-15 asserted
    `Assert.Equal(sent.PayloadSha256, file.FileDownloadName == null ? null : sent.PayloadSha256)`
    — a comparison of the recorded digest WITH ITSELF whenever the download carried a
    file name. It reads as "the served bytes match the recorded digest" and could not
    fail for any payload, which is the seventeenth unfalsifiable assertion of the hunt
    and the one shape this suite exists to refuse.
    
    Measured rather than argued: appending one byte to the payload served by
    WorkforceTimesheetService.DownloadBatchAsync leaves the old line GREEN, while
    WorkforceTimesheetTests.Exporting_renders_the_frozen_snapshot_and_the_stored_bytes_are_what_the_download_serves
    reds on the same mutation — so the bytes really did diverge and this step slept
    through it.
    
    The digest is now recomputed here from what was served. Under that same mutation the
    line reds naming both digests; a second mutation nulling FileName reds the adjacent
    name assertion, so neither new line is a second tautology.
    
    Not deleted in favour of the sibling check: that one covers the ordinary download,
    whereas WFJ-15's sentence is that the kill switch leaves the artifact
    BYTE-IDENTICAL, which no other test states.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>

diff --git a/WebApi.Tests/Workforce/WorkforceEndToEndJourneyTests.cs b/WebApi.Tests/Workforce/WorkforceEndToEndJourneyTests.cs
